# OneKey Hardware SDK Example Project - Coding Rules

## Project Overview
This is a React + TypeScript web application for testing and demonstrating OneKey hardware wallet SDK functionality. The project uses modern web technologies with a focus on clean, maintainable code and consistent user experience.

## Tech Stack & Dependencies
- **Framework:** React 18+ with TypeScript
- **Routing:** React Router DOM
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS with custom design system
- **UI Components:** Radix UI primitives with custom wrappers
- **Icons:** Lucide React
- **Internationalization:** i18next with react-i18next
- **Hardware SDK:** @onekeyfe/hd-core for hardware wallet integration
- **State Management:** Zustand with persistence middleware
- **Build Tool:** Vite
- **Component Variants:** Class Variance Authority (CVA)
- **Compression:** LZ-string for log data compression

## Architecture Overview

### Component Architecture
The project uses a unified component architecture:

```
UnifiedMethodExecutor (Core Implementation)
├── type="standard" (Standard method execution)
└── type="firmware" (Firmware method execution)
```

**Key Components:**
- `UnifiedMethodExecutor.tsx` - Core execution logic for all hardware methods
- `DeviceInteractionArea.tsx` - Device animation and interaction UI
- `ExecutionPanel.tsx` - Request/response display and logging
- `UnifiedLogger.tsx` - Centralized logging system

### Persistent Log Storage System
- **Storage Backend:** Zustand persist middleware with localStorage
- **Compression:** LZ-string compression for large log entries (>1KB)
- **Limits:** Configurable entry count, size, and expiration limits
- **UI Filtering:** Automatic filtering of redundant device fields in UI events
- **Statistics:** Real-time storage statistics and compression metrics

### Hardware SDK Integration
- **Cancel Functionality:** Proper SDK cancel method integration for operation cancellation
- **Device Management:** Centralized device state management
- **Error Handling:** Comprehensive error handling with user feedback

## Code Style & Patterns

### React Components
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Prefer named exports over default exports for components
- Use React hooks for state management and side effects
- Follow the pattern: `const ComponentName: React.FC<Props> = ({ ...props }) => { ... }`
- Always define TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Use descriptive prop names and include JSDoc comments for complex props

### TypeScript Guidelines
- Use strict TypeScript configuration
- Define interfaces for all data structures
- Use union types for component variants and states
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use generic types for reusable components
- Always type function parameters and return values

### File Organization
```
app/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/           # Base UI components (Button, Card, Dialog, etc.)
│   ├── common/       # Shared business components
│   └── layout/       # Layout components
├── routes/           # Page components following file-based routing
├── hooks/            # Custom React hooks
├── store/            # Zustand state management
├── services/         # API and hardware service integrations
├── data/             # Static data and method configurations
├── utils/            # Utility functions
└── types/            # TypeScript type definitions
```

### Naming Conventions
- **Components:** PascalCase (e.g., `UnifiedMethodExecutor`, `DeviceInteractionArea`)
- **Files:** kebab-case for routes, PascalCase for components
- **Variables/Functions:** camelCase
- **Constants:** UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- **CSS Classes:** Tailwind utility classes, kebab-case for custom classes
- **Interfaces:** PascalCase with `I` prefix for complex interfaces (e.g., `IMethodConfig`)

## UI Component Library

### Design System
- **Default Theme:** Dark theme with modern neutral palette
- **Typography:** Clean, readable fonts with consistent sizing
- **Spacing:** Consistent spacing scale using Tailwind's spacing system
- **Borders:** Rounded corners (rounded-xl, rounded-2xl) for modern look
- **Shadows:** Elegant shadow system with hover effects
- **Animations:** Subtle transitions and hover effects (duration-200, duration-300)

### Component Patterns

#### Button Component
```tsx
// Use the custom Button component with consistent variants
<Button variant="default" size="lg" className="...">
  Action Text
</Button>

// Available variants: default, destructive, warning, outline, secondary, ghost, link, success, elegant
// Available sizes: sm, default, lg, icon, xl
```

#### Card Component
```tsx
// Use Card components for content grouping
<Card className="bg-card border border-border/50">
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Title</CardTitle>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    Content here
  </CardContent>
</Card>
```

#### Layout Patterns
- Use `PageLayout` for consistent page structure
- Use `fixedHeight={true}` for full-screen layouts
- Follow the grid system: `grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-4`
- Use flexbox for component internal layouts

### Responsive Design
- Mobile-first approach with Tailwind breakpoints
- Use `lg:` prefix for desktop-specific styles
- Ensure components work on all screen sizes
- Test layouts at different viewport sizes

## Hardware SDK Integration

### Device Connection Patterns
```tsx
// Always check device connection before operations
const { currentDevice, sdkInitState } = useDeviceStore();

if (!currentDevice) {
  return <DeviceNotConnectedState showFullPage={true} />;
}

if (!sdkInitState.isInitialized) {
  // Show loading or initialization state
}
```

### Method Execution Pattern
```tsx
// Use UnifiedMethodExecutor for consistent method execution
<UnifiedMethodExecutor
  methodConfig={selectedMethod}
  executionHandler={handleMethodExecution}
  type="standard" // or "firmware"
  className="h-full"
/>
```

### Cancel Operation Implementation
```tsx
// Implement proper cancel functionality
const handleCancel = async () => {
  if (status === 'loading' || status === 'device-interaction') {
    const { cancelHardwareOperation } = await import('../../services/hardwareService');
    const connectId = currentDevice?.connectId;
    const result = await cancelHardwareOperation(connectId);
    
    if (result.success) {
      // Handle successful cancellation
    }
  }
};
```

### Error Handling
- Use toast notifications for user feedback
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Log errors for debugging
- Provide meaningful error messages to users

## State Management with Zustand

### Store Structure
```tsx
// Use Zustand with persistence for device state
export const useDeviceStore = create<DeviceState>()(
  persist(
    (set, get) => ({
      // Device management
      currentDevice: null,
      connectedDevices: [],
      
      // Log management with compression
      logs: [],
      logStorageConfig: DEFAULT_LOG_CONFIG,
      
      // Actions
      addLog: (log) => { /* Implementation */ },
      clearLogs: () => { /* Implementation */ },
      getLogStorageStats: () => { /* Implementation */ },
    }),
    {
      name: 'onekey-device-store',
      version: 1,
      partialize: (state) => ({
        logs: state.logs.map(log => compressLogEntry(log, state.logStorageConfig)),
        logStorageConfig: state.logStorageConfig,
      }),
    }
  )
);
```

### Log Storage Best Practices
- **Compression:** Use LZ-string for entries >1KB
- **Filtering:** Remove redundant device fields from UI events
- **Limits:** Respect configured size, count, and expiration limits
- **Statistics:** Provide real-time storage statistics

## Routing & Navigation

### Route Structure
- Use file-based routing pattern
- Keep route components focused and delegate to business components
- Use consistent breadcrumb navigation
- Implement proper loading and error states

### Page Layout Consistency
```tsx
// Standard page structure
<PageLayout fixedHeight={true}>
  <div className="h-full flex flex-col">
    <div className="flex-1 flex flex-col px-4 py-2 min-h-0">
      {/* Breadcrumb navigation - fixed height */}
      <div className="flex-shrink-0 mb-3">
        <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbItems} />
      </div>
      
      {/* Main content - fills remaining space */}
      <div className="flex-1 min-h-0">
        {/* Content here */}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
```

## Specific Component Requirements

### Method Execution Pages
- **Use UnifiedMethodExecutor** - Follow the unified execution pattern
- **Proper cancel functionality** - Implement SDK cancel method integration
- **File selection capability** - Implement file picker for firmware update methods
- **Consistent layout** - Use standard page layout with breadcrumbs

### File Selection Implementation
```tsx
// For methods requiring file uploads, implement file selection
const handleFileSelect = async () => {
  const fileInput = document.createElement('input');
  fileInput.type = 'file';
  fileInput.accept = '.bin,.zip'; // Appropriate file types
  fileInput.onchange = (event) => {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (file) {
      // Update method parameters with selected file
      setMethodParameters({ ...methodParameters, firmwareFile: file });
    }
  };
  fileInput.click();
};
```

### Log Management
- **Simplified Statistics:** Only show total entries and log size
- **Real-time Updates:** Update statistics as logs are added/removed
- **Export Functionality:** Support JSON and text export formats
- **Configuration Management:** Allow users to configure storage limits

## Performance Considerations

### Log Storage Performance
- **Compression Thresholds:** Only compress data >1KB
- **Batch Operations:** Process multiple logs efficiently
- **Memory Management:** Clean up expired logs automatically
- **UI Responsiveness:** Avoid blocking UI during compression

### Component Optimization
- **Lazy Loading:** Use dynamic imports for heavy components
- **Memoization:** Use React.memo and useMemo appropriately
- **Virtual Scrolling:** For large log lists
- **Debounced Updates:** For real-time statistics

## Development Guidelines

### Code Quality
- Follow ESLint and TypeScript strict mode
- Write meaningful commit messages
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Implement proper error boundaries

### Testing Strategy
- Test hardware SDK integration thoroughly
- Verify log compression and decompression
- Test cancel functionality across different states
- Validate responsive design on multiple devices

### Documentation
- Keep README.md updated with setup instructions
- Document complex business logic
- Maintain type definitions for all interfaces
- Update this .cursorrules file as architecture evolves

## Security Considerations
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize log data before storage
- Handle sensitive device information carefully
- Implement proper error handling to avoid information leakage
